Describe: the better prompt

Review the code in logit.R and tell me what it does, step by step. Don’t assume the code matches the comments. Don’t change anything yet.

What qwen did (26 seconds): nothing. It wrote a tool call as text and stopped.
What it said:
I'll review the code in logit.R step by step to understand what it does. Let me first read the file.
&lt;function=read&gt;
&lt;parameter=filePath&gt;
/private/tmp/agent-trials/z-qwen/print-better-r1/logit.R
&lt;/parameter&gt;
&lt;/function&gt;
&lt;/tool_call&gt;
In 10 runs:

8 read the file and still called the output a probability.
2 stopped like this one.

(Back to the choices.)
qwen needs 32 GB of memory and about 25 GB of free disk space.
Install Ollama, qwen, and OpenCode. Paste the block into PowerShell (Windows) or Terminal (Mac). The model download takes a while.
On Windows:
irm https://ollama.com/install.ps1 | iex
ollama pull qwen3-coder:30b
winget install SST.opencode --accept-source-agreements --accept-package-agreements
New-Item -ItemType Directory -Force "$HOME\.config\opencode" | Out-Null
@'
{
  "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json",
  "provider": {
    "ollama": {
      "npm": "@ai-sdk/openai-compatible",
      "name": "Ollama (local)",
      "options": { "baseURL": "http://localhost:11434/v1" },
      "models": {
        "qwen3-coder:30b": { "name": "Qwen3-Coder 30B (local)" }
      }
    }
  }
}
'@ | Set-Content "$HOME\.config\opencode\opencode.json"
On Mac:
curl -fsSL https://ollama.com/install.sh | sh
ollama pull qwen3-coder:30b
curl -fsSL https://opencode.ai/install | bash
mkdir -p ~/.config/opencode
cat &gt; ~/.config/opencode/opencode.json &lt;&lt;'EOF'
{
  "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json",
  "provider": {
    "ollama": {
      "npm": "@ai-sdk/openai-compatible",
      "name": "Ollama (local)",
      "options": { "baseURL": "http://localhost:11434/v1" },
      "models": {
        "qwen3-coder:30b": { "name": "Qwen3-Coder 30B (local)" }
      }
    }
  }
}
EOF
(It may ask for your Mac password; the typing doesn’t show.)
Set the context length. Click the Ollama icon (in the menu bar on a Mac, or the system tray on Windows) &gt; Settings, and move the Context length slider to 64k.
Check. Open a new terminal window and run
opencode run --model ollama/qwen3-coder:30b "Reply with only the word hello."
It should print hello. (It can take a minute or two.)
Then run ollama ps and make sure the CONTEXT column says 65536. Then go to A First Example.
If you get stuck, copy the command and everything it printed and send it to Bryson or me.

